# attack — fail
#
# The attack verb attempted and missed: the checks pass, the dice do not, and the outcome block
# reports FAILURE. The fight still starts — a swing seen is a swing seen — but it starts with the
# enemy holding the initiative and with nothing struck and nothing learned.
#
# Reached in 12/12 of the sampled location ids (see --verb-probe). --location-type and
# --location-id decide the whole scene, so this does not depend on world generation; --npc-static
# pins people to one room; --observe-only pins the phase to one object, which is why the keyword is
# clicked by index (which word the prose highlights is not something a test can know).
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Hall" --period dawn --observe-only "brewer"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

strategy fail-dice
goal attack
advance 12 180

click keyword 0
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
regions

click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180

expect FAILURE

# What makes this an attack test rather than an any-verb test: SUCCESS/FAILURE is the outcome banner
# of every verb alike, so only this can tell them apart.
expect-verb attack

# The failure assertion that means something: no blow landed. The fight is the miss's consequence
# and happens either way, so it is first_blow — not fight_trigger — that separates a hit from a miss.
expect-no-outcome first_blow
expect-not First blow:
# Nothing was struck, so nothing was learned. The lesson rides on the blow.
expect-no-outcome modus_mentis_grant
# And nobody in the scene is carrying a mark: read as a negative over every NPC at once, so it does
# not depend on which of them the phase happened to open on or on the order of the fields.
expect-no-state npcs wounds=1

click button
wait mode Fighting 60
quit
